  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>An&nbsp;electric dipole is a system of two charges of equal and opposite magnitude placed at a short distance.</p><p>The dipole moment is,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>q</mi><mi>l</mi></math>.</p><p>The electric field due to the dipole is,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mi>p</mi></mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mfrac></math></p><p>Clearly, the electric field varies as&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mfrac></math>.</p></blockquote>",
